403,"lo":-21.938323,"d":299},{"n":"Reykjavik Museum of Photography","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":64.149308,"lo":-21.941348,"d":306,"h":"Mo-Th 10:00-18:00; Fr 11:00-18:00; Sa, Su 13:00-17:00","w":"https://borgarsogusafn.is/ljosmyndasafn","wp":"en:Reykjavík Museum of Photography","ds":"Reykjavík Museum of Photography (Icelandic: Ljósmyndasafn Reykjavíkur [ˈljouːsˌmɪntaˌsapn ˈreiːcaˌviːkʏr̥]), in Reykjavík, Iceland, preserves about five million photographs by professional and amateur photographers, from around 1870 to the present century. The collection includes studio portraits, and industrial, advertising, press, landscape and family photographs.","wpu":"Reykjavík Museum of Photography","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/The_exhibition_-_Children.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Flæði","g":"sights","k":"gallery","la":64.149136,"lo":-21.941076,"d":322},{"n":"Reykjavik Art Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":64.149065,"lo":-21.940444,"d":327,"h":"Mo-We 10:00-17:00; Th 10:00-22:00; Fr-Su 10:00-17:00","w":"https://listasafnreykjavikur.is/","wp":"en:Reykjavik Art Museum","ds":"Reykjavik Art Museum (Icelandic: Listasafn Reykjavíkur [ˈlɪstaˌsapn ˌreiːcaˌviːkʏr̥]; founded in 1973) is the largest visual art institution in Iceland.","wpu":"Reykjavik Art Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/ListasafnReykjavikur.JPG/960px-ListasafnReykjavikur.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"The Reykjavik EDITION","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":64.149791,"lo":-21.935328,"d":334,"w":"https://www.editionhotels.com/reykjavik/","ph":"+354 582 0000","wifi":1},{"n":"Kogga Ceramic Studio","g":"sights","k":"gallery","la":64.148993,"lo":-21.942277,"d":352},{"n":"Óðinn","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":64.153238,"lo":-21.947098,"d":370,"wp":"en:ICGV Óðinn","ds":"ICGV Óðinn is a decommissioned offshore patrol vessel formerly operated by the Icelandic Coast Guard. She is the oldest ship in the coastguard's fleet, and it is believed that her Burmeister & Wain engines are the only such engines that are still serviceable in the world today. Since her withdrawal from active duty, she has served as a museum ship at the Reykjavík Maritime Museum in Reykjavík Harbour.","wpu":"ICGV Óðinn","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Odinn.JPG/960px-Odinn.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Butterfly Guesthouse","g":"sights","k":"hostel","la":64.149502,"lo":-21.945177,"d":374,"w":"https://butterfly.is/","ph":"+354 894 1864","wifi":1},{"n":"Volcano Express","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":64.150279,"lo":-21.933307,"d":376,"h":"Mo-Su 10:00-20:00","w":"https://www.volcanoexpress.is/","ph":"+3545285050"},{"n":"Lighthouse Apartments","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":64.149435,"lo":-21.945699,"d":397,"wifi":1},{"n":"Mound","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":64.154402,"lo":-21.93381,"d":401},{"n":"Steinbryggjan","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":64.148468,"lo":-21.93798,"d":404,"wp":"is:Steinbryggjan"},{"n":"CenterHotel Plaza","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":64.148291,"lo":-21.941746,"d":421,"ph":"+354 595 8550","wifi":1},{"n":"Radisson Blu 1919 Hotel, Reykjavík","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":64.148205,"lo":-21.938593,"d":427,"w":"https://www.radissonhotels.com/en-us/hotels/radisson-blu-reykjavik-1919","wifi":1},{"n":"Reykjavík Maritime Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":64.153311,"lo":-21.948576,"d":440,"w":"https://borgarsogusafn.is/","wp":"is:Sjóminjasafnið í Reykjavík","ds":"The Reykjavik Maritime Museum (Icelandic: Sjóminjasafnið í Reykjavík [ˈsjouːˌmɪnjaˌsapnɪð i ˈreiːcaˌviːk]), formerly Víkin Maritime Museum, is a maritime museum located by the old harbour in the capital of Iceland, Reykjavík and run by Reykjavik City. The museum was established in 2005, and it is now one of five sites belonging to Reykjavik City Museum.","wpu":"Reykjavík Maritime Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Reykjav%C3%ADk_Maritime_Museum%2C_2020.jpg/960px-Reykjav%C3%ADk_Maritime_Museum%2C_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"The Icelandic Phallological Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":64.148468,"lo":-21.93576,"d":443,"h":"10:00-19:00","w":"https://www.phallus.is/","wp":"en:Icelandic Phallological Museum","ds":"The Icelandic Phallological Museum (Icelandic: Hið íslenzka reðasafn [ˈhɪːð ˈistlɛnska ˈrɛːðaˌsapn̥]), located in Reykjavík, Iceland, houses the world's largest display of penises and penile parts. It holds over 300 penises from more than 100 species of mammals. The museum also holds 22 imagined penises of creatures from Icelandic folklore.","wpu":"Icelandic Phallological Museum"},{"n":"Víkin","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":64.153187,"lo":-21.94888,"d":450,"h":"Mo-Su 10:00-17:00","w":"http://www.sjominjasafn.is","wp":"en:Reykjavík Maritime Museum","ds":"The Reykjavik Maritime Museum (Icelandic: Sjóminjasafnið í Reykjavík [ˈsjouːˌmɪnjaˌsapnɪð i ˈreiːcaˌviːk]), formerly Víkin Maritime Museum, is a maritime museum located by the old harbour in the capital of Iceland, Reykjavík and run by Reykjavik City. The museum was established in 2005, and it is now one of five sites belonging to Reykjavik City Museum.","wpu":"Reykjavík Maritime Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Reykjav%C3%ADk_Maritime_Museum%2C_2020.jpg/960px-Reykjav%C3%ADk_Maritime_Museum%2C_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"The Marshall House","g":"sights","k":"gallery","la":64.156131,"lo":-21.938979,"d":462,"h":"We-Su 12:00-18:00","w":"https://marshallhusid.is/"},{"n":"Aðalstræti 10","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":64.147836,"lo":-21.942136,"d":474,"h":"Mo-Su 10:00-17:00","w":"https://borgarsogusafn.is/is/borgarsogusafn-reykjavikur/um-borgarsogusafn/frettir/adalstraeti-10-elsta-hus-midborgarinnar-opnad"