":"Mo-Su 10:00-23:00","w":"https://www.bonus.is/"},{"n":"Udafoss","g":"essentials","k":"laundry","la":64.14383,"lo":-21.922071,"d":1257,"h":"Mo-Fr 08:00-18:00","w":"https://www.udafoss.is/","ph":"+354 551 2301"},{"n":"Melabúðin","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":64.144713,"lo":-21.960361,"d":1277,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-20:00; Sa 10:00-20:00; Su 10:00-20:00"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":64.143999,"lo":-21.920049,"d":1314},{"n":"Penninn / Eymundsson","g":"essentials","k":"books","la":64.143965,"lo":-21.919964,"d":1319,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-22:00; Sa-Su 10:00-22:00","w":"https://www.eymundsson.is/","ph":"+354 540 2000","wifi":1},{"n":"Austurvöllur","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.1472,"lo":-21.9398,"d":533,"ds":"Austurvöllur (Icelandic pronunciation: [ˈœystʏrˌvœtlʏr̥]) is a public square in Reykjavík, Iceland. The square is a popular gathering place for the citizens of Reykjavík, and especially during good weather due to the prevalence of cafés on Vallarstræti and Pósthússtræti. It has also been a focal point of protests, as it is near the Parliament of Iceland.","wpu":"Austurvöllur","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Austurv%C3%B6llur_2023.jpg/960px-Austurv%C3%B6llur_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Whales of Iceland","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.1556,"lo":-21.9488,"d":584,"ds":"Whales of Iceland is a natural history museum located in the Grandi harbour district of Reykjavík, Iceland. First opened in 2015, the museum is dedicated to educating visitors about the various cetacean species that have been sighted in Icelandic waters throughout recorded history.","wpu":"Whales of Iceland","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Whales_of_Iceland_Interior.jpg/960px-Whales_of_Iceland_Interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Christ the King Cathedral (Reykjavík)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.1475,"lo":-21.9485,"d":648,"ds":"Landakotskirkja (transl. Landakot's Church), officially named Basilika Krists konungs (transl. The Basilica of Christ the King) and often referred to as Kristskirkja (transl. Christ's Church), is a Catholic basilica in the western part of the Icelandic capital of Reykjavík and is the cathedral of the Diocese of Reykjavík. It is the sole Catholic cathedral in Iceland.","wpu":"Christ the King Cathedral (Reykjavík)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Landakotskirkja_-_panoramio_%283%29.jpg/960px-Landakotskirkja_-_panoramio_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"National Gallery of Iceland","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.144111,"lo":-21.93925,"d":877,"ds":"The National Gallery of Iceland (Icelandic: Listasafn Íslands [ˈlɪstaˌsapn ˈistlan(t)s]) is an art museum in Reykjavík which contains a collection of Icelandic art. The gallery features artwork of famous Icelandic artists and artwork that helps explain the traditional Icelandic culture.","wpu":"National Gallery of Iceland","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/National_Art_Gallery_of_Iceland.jpg/960px-National_Art_Gallery_of_Iceland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Vatnsstígur 16-18","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.147014,"lo":-21.924344,"d":939,"ds":"Vatnsstígur 16–18 is a residential tower located in downtown Reykjavík, Iceland. It is a part of a larger project called \"shadow district\" (Icelandic: Skuggahverfi) under phase 2. This tower is the tallest residential tower in Reykjavík, standing 69.35 m (227.5 ft) tall.","wpu":"Vatnsstígur 16-18","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/5a/Vatnsst%C3%ADgur_18-Reykjavik.jpg/960px-Vatnsst%C3%ADgur_18-Reykjavik.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"National Museum of Iceland","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.141944,"lo":-21.948056,"d":1184,"ds":"The National Museum of Iceland (Icelandic: Þjóðminjasafn Íslands, [ˈθjouðˌmɪnjaˌsapn ˈistlan(t)s]) is a museum in Reykjavík, Iceland. It was established on 24 February 1863, with Jón Árnason the first curator of the Icelandic collection, previously kept in Danish museums.","wpu":"National Museum of Iceland","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Nationalmuseum_20120919_2.jpg/960px-Nationalmuseum_20120919_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Statue of Leif Erikson (Reykjavík)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.142353,"lo":-21.927544,"d":1231,"ds":"Leifr Eiricsson, sometimes called the Leif Eiricsson Memorial, is a statue of Norse explorer Leif Erikson created by American artist Alexander Stirling Calder. The artwork was commissioned by the United States government as a gift to the Icelandic people for the 1,000th anniversary of the Alþingi in 1930. The statue was unveiled on July 17, 1932, in Reykjavík, Iceland atop a hill overlooking the city.","wpu":"Statue of Leif Erikson (Reykjavík)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Leif_Erikson_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Leif_Erikson_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hallgrímskirkja","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.1417,"lo":-21.9266,"d":1316,"ds":"Hallgrímskirkja (Icelandic pronunciation: [ˈhatl̥ˌkrimsˌcʰɪr̥ca], Church of Hallgrímur) is a Protestant Lutheran (Church of Iceland) parish church in Reykjavík, Iceland. At 74.5 metres (244 ft) tall, it is the largest church in Iceland and second tallest building in the country.","wpu":"Hallgrímskirkja","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Hallgrimskirkja_mai_2026.jpg/960px-Hallgrimskirkja_mai_2026.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sundhöll Reykjavíkur","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.1417,"lo":-21.9203,"d":1491,"ds":"Sundhöll Reykjavíkur (lit. \"Reykjavík Swimming Palace\", often shortened to Sundhöllin, but that name can also apply to other indoor swimming pools in Iceland), is the oldest purpose-built indoor swimming pool in Reykjavík. It was designed by the Icelandic architect Guðjón Samúelsson and opened in 1937 at Barónsstígur, close to the elementary school Austurbæjarskóli.","wpu":"Sundhöll Reykjavíkur","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Sundh%C3%B6ll_Reykjav%C3%ADkur_%285201827496%29.jpg/960px-Sundh%C3%B6ll_Reykjav%C3%ADkur_%285201827496%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Árnagarður","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.138889,"lo":-21.951389,"d":1558,"ds":"Árnagarður (Icelandic pronunciation: [ˈau(r)tnaˌkarðʏr̥], lit. 'Árni's Garden') is a building in Reykjavík, Iceland, located on the campus of the University of Iceland. It is named after the 18th century scholar Árni Magnússon, and houses the Árni Magnússon Institute for Icelandic Studies. It opened on 21 December 1969.","wpu":"Árnagarður","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Arnagardur.JPG/960px-Arnagardur.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Askja (building)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":64.137222,"lo":-21.945833,"d":1667,"ds":"Askja ([ˈasca] ) is a building on the campus of the University of Iceland (Sturlugata 7, 101 Reykjavík), named after the volcano Askja. It primarily houses the departments of biology and geosciences. It was designed by architect Maggi Jónsson.","wpu":"Askja (building)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9e/University_of_Iceland-Askja.jpg/960px-University_of_Iceland-Askja.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Reykjavík is the capital and largest city of Iceland. It is located on the southern shore of the Faxaflói bay in southwest Iceland and has a latitude of 64°08′ N, making it the world's northernmost capital of a sovereign state. Reykjavík has a population of around 140,000 as of 2026, and the surrounding Capital Region has a population of around 252,000, constituting approximately 64% of Iceland's population.","wpu":"Reykjavík","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Reykjav%C3%ADk%2C_view_from_Hallgr%C3%ADmskirkja_%282%29.jpg/960px-Reykjav%C3%ADk%2C_view_from_Hallgr%C3%ADmskirkja_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}